---
import './HowToAccess.css';
import site from '../data/site.json';

/**
 * HowToAccess.astro — §1 "Explanation Wrongness" (user review 2026-10-19)
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Introduces the idea of wrongness exactly like the source material:
 * the motivating example that sets up "wrong — but how?"
 *
 * The step-by-step assessment ladder ("every scenario is assessed the
 * same way") moved to HeroSection.astro per user review 2026-10-29, so
 * it sits directly under the "How to Assess Wrongness" title it details.
 *
 * Security: static content, auto-escaped.
 */
---

<section id="how-to-access" class="section" aria-labelledby="access-title">
	<div class="container">
		<h2 id="access-title">Explanation Wrongness</h2>
		<p class="intro">
			Explanations can be wrong — but &ldquo;wrong&rdquo; is not one thing. 
			<!-- The	scenarios in this page describe hypothetical situations in which a
			stakeholder engages with an explanation of a concrete AI model. Each
			scenario is self-contained, so models and explanations vary between
			scenarios. -->
		</p>

		<aside class="teaser" data-reveal aria-label="Motivating example">
			<blockquote>
				<p>
					&ldquo;The applicant was rejected <strong>because job experience is too
					high</strong>.&rdquo;
				</p>
			</blockquote>
			<p class="teaser-question">
				You suspect this cannot be right. But is it wrong because the applicant
				should have been accepted? Because that is not how the model works?
				Or both?
			</p>
			<p class="teaser-answer">
				It depends which reference point you use to judge the explanation — but how do we assess wrongness systematically?
			</p>
		</aside>
	</div>
</section>


</style>

